        %span.os OS
      %a{href: "#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}tutorials/", class: "active"}= nav_tutorials
      %a{href: "#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}hardware/"}= nav_hardware
      %a{href: "#{asset_prefix}#{lang_dir}documentation/"}= nav_documentation
    %main.sub-main
      .sub-content
        %p.tutorial-number= tut_card_first_number
        %h1= first_h1
        %p.sub-lead= first_lead

        %h2= first_h2_center
        %p
          Power the receiver with the radio <em>bound</em> but sticks neutral. Every servo must sit at <strong>1500&nbsp;µs</strong>. If a horn is not perpendicular to the linkage, unscrew it and re-seat it on the closest spline — never force the neutral with sub-trim on the radio; the flight controller already assumes a true 1500&nbsp;µs centre.
        %p
          The wing pair is the critical one: both wings must be at the <em>same</em> geometric angle at neutral. A half-tooth difference between left and right is visible as a roll bias in flight and cannot be trimmed away by the gyro without eating into its authority.

        %h2= first_h2_direction
        %table
          %tr
            %th Stick
            %th Expected surface
          %tr
            %td Roll right
            %td Right wing tips up, left wing tips down
          %tr
            %td Pitch up (pull)
            %td Both wings tilt to raise the nose (or elevon equivalent)
          %tr
            %td Yaw right
            %td Crest rudder deflects right
        %p
          If a surface moves the wrong way, reverse it <em>in the flight controller config</em> (the servo kernel direction flags), not by flipping the servo or using a radio channel reverse that shifts the 988–2012&nbsp;µs map. Keep the radio output symmetric around 1500&nbsp;µs.

        %h2= first_h2_endpoints
        %p
          The servo kernel defaults to <code>servoMinUs</code>&nbsp;988 and <code>servoMaxUs</code>&nbsp;2012. Set mechanical endpoints so the wing sweeps its full travel <em>without binding</em> at the extremes. Listen for the buzz of a stalled servo — back off the endpoint until it stops. Endpoints live in the servo kernel, not in the radio: leave the radio at ±100%.

        %h2= first_h2_preflight
        %ul
          %li Wings centred at neutral, both directions correct.
          %li Endpoints set with no stall buzz.
          %li Failsafe verified — transmitter off → wings and rudder centre.
          %li Gyro responds in the correct sense (roll the airframe, rudder should oppose).
          %li Battery voltage matches the BEC rating; servos do not brown-out on a full sweep.

        %h2= first_h2_maiden
        %p
          Hand-launch into a gentle glide first, <em>motor/flap off</em>, to confirm trim and balance. Only when it glides straight should you engage a low flap frequency and low ferocity. Increase frequency slowly; resonance and aeroelastic effects change with airspeed, so re-trim after each change. Land early on the first battery — the goal is data, not duration.

